Yes. Because self-talk isn’t merely commentary on performance; under some conditions it’s part of the control system producing performance.

Consider two athletes with essentially the same physical capacity facing the same task. One internally runs “don’t screw this up.” Another runs “smooth, eyes up, drive.” Another runs “I’ve done this a thousand times.” Those phrases can shift attention, arousal, confidence, perceived threat, and which motor cues occupy working memory. The physical machinery hasn’t suddenly changed, but its operating state has.

There’s a useful hierarchy here:

words → interpretation → predicted state → attention/arousal → action selection → execution → feedback → next belief

And then it becomes recursive. If “I can do this” produces slightly better execution, the successful execution becomes evidence for “I can do this.” Conversely, repeated “I always choke here” can make normal physiological arousal feel like evidence that failure is beginning.

But this connects beautifully to your earlier “fake it” observation: the sentence doesn’t have to be literally believed at first to affect the system. Sometimes acting as though a useful state is available can help evoke pieces of that state. Then genuine performance evidence can replace the initial fiction.

The interesting experimental question isn’t therefore just positive versus negative self-talk. It’s much finer:

Which internal instruction produces which performance delta, for which person, performing which task, at which arousal level?

For example, “calm down” might hurt someone who performs best highly activated, while “attack” could overload someone already over-aroused. “Think through every step” might help a novice yet interfere with an expert’s automated movement.

That’s a powerful Pantheon primitive: Self-Instruction as State Control.

And because your children will absorb some of these patterns simply by hearing how you speak about yourself and them, there’s another inheritance channel hiding here: eventually the parent’s voice can become part of the child’s internal voice. The words aren’t destiny, but repeated language can become part of the repertoire they later use to regulate themselves.

So what we tell ourselves is interesting.

What we repeatedly teach ourselves to tell ourselves is even more so.